@*
    The Demo Lab, at "/lab" and "/lab/{scenarioId}".

    WHY IT EXISTS. This repository claims a set of invariants. The suite proves them on every push,
    but a reviewer with ten minutes will not run a test suite and a README assertion is worth
    approximately nothing. So this page lets a stranger press a button and watch an invariant hold
    against the same code a purchase runs through: the HTTP that went out, what came back, how long
    it took, and what changed in the database. It is only worth having if it is honest, which is why
    every step carries its own fidelity and why a verdict that did not hold is rendered exactly as
    loudly as one that did.

    THE FIRST-PAINT RULE STILL HOLDS. The catalog browses, searches, filters and sorts from a static
    file with the API and the database switched off. This page is not on that path: nothing here is
    fetched until it has already painted, and a shopper who never opens the lab wakes nothing. It is
    linked from the footer rather than the shelf navigation, because it is for a reviewer and not
    for somebody buying rope.

    THE PERMALINK IS THE POINT OF THE ROUTE. "/lab/oversell" is a link that goes in a CV, a README
    or a job application and opens on one demonstration, described, ready to run. So the scenario's
    title is this page's h1 on that route rather than a generic one.

    NOTHING ABOUT A SCENARIO IS WRITTEN DOWN HERE. The claim, the invariant, the enforcing statement
    and the test file all come from GET /api/demo/lab/scenarios, which is the server's own
    catalogue. A storefront that restated them would be the thing that made a button's label
    disagree with what the button does.
*@
@page "/lab/{ScenarioId?}"
@using System.Diagnostics
@using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection
@using VelaCommerce.Storefront.Lab
@implements IDisposable
@inject HttpClient Http
@inject IServiceProvider Services

<PageTitle>@DocumentTitle</PageTitle>

<nav class="vc-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a class="vc-crumbs__link" href="">Catalog</a>
    <span class="vc-crumbs__sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    @if (Focused is { } crumb)
    {
        <a class="vc-crumbs__link" href="lab">Demo lab</a>
        <span class="vc-crumbs__sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span>@crumb.Heading</span>
    }
    else
    {
        <span>Demo lab</span>
    }
</nav>

<div class="vc-hero">
    @if (Focused is { } scenario)
    {
        <span class="vc-eyebrow">Demo lab &middot; one demonstration</span>
        <h1 class="vc-section-title">@scenario.Heading</h1>
        <p class="vc-lede">@scenario.Claim</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <span class="vc-eyebrow">Demo lab</span>
        <h1 class="vc-section-title">Press a button, watch an invariant hold</h1>
        <p class="vc-lede">
            Every claim this shop makes about overselling, double-clicks and duplicate settlements
            is proved by a test. Tests are not something you are going to run. So each of these
            buttons runs the same demonstration against the live shop and hands back the transcript:
            every request, every answer, the milliseconds, and the rows afterwards.
        </p>
    }
</div>

@* The honesty statement, above everything, on both routes. It is the claim the rest of the page
   depends on being true, so it is not tucked into a disclosure. *@
<div class="vc-notice vc-lab-honesty">
    <p class="vc-notice__title">This runs against the shop, not against a mock</p>
    <p class="vc-notice__body">
        Every shopper below is a real HTTP request from the API to its own listening address: a real
        session cookie minted by the real middleware, a real cart, a real checkout, the same guarded
        <code class="vc-code">UPDATE</code> and the same signed webhook a purchase goes through.
        Nothing calls an internal method with a hand-made session, and where a step is anything less
        than a live exchange it says so on itself rather than in a footnote.
    </p>
    @if (Catalog?.StockPolicy is { Length: > 0 } policy)
    {
        <p class="vc-notice__body">@policy</p>
    }
</div>

@if (Catalog is null && _catalogLoading)
{
    <div class="vc-waking" role="status" aria-live="polite">
        <span class="vc-waking__pip" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="vc-waking__body">
            <p class="vc-waking__title">@(_catalogWaking ? "Waking the shop up" : "Fetching the lab's catalogue")</p>
            <p class="vc-waking__note">
                @if (ScenarioId is { Length: > 0 } pending)
                {
                    <text>
                        Reading what <code class="vc-code">@pending</code> will do. The description of
                        a scenario is served by the API rather than baked into this page, so a button
                        and what the button does cannot disagree — which means there is nothing
                        truthful to show until the shop answers.
                    </text>
                }
                else
                {
                    <text>
                        The API sleeps when nobody is buying, which is what keeps it free to run. The
                        first call after a quiet spell waits for a container to start and a database
                        to resume. The catalog you were just browsing is a static file and never
                        needed any of this.
                    </text>
                }
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
}
else if (Catalog is null && _state.CatalogFailure is { } failure)
{
    <div class="vc-alert vc-alert--error" role="alert">
        <h2 class="vc-alert__title">@failure.Message</h2>
        <p class="vc-alert__body">
            The lab is the one part of this build that cannot work without the API — a scenario is
            run <em>by</em> the shop, so there is nothing to demonstrate while the shop is
            unreachable. Nothing else is affected: the catalog is a static file and browsing,
            searching and sorting all still work.
        </p>
        @if (failure.Detail is { Length: > 0 } detail)
        {
            <details class="vc-alert__detail">
                <summary>Technical detail</summary>
                @detail
            </details>
        }
        <div class="vc-alert__actions">
            <button type="button" class="vc-btn vc-btn--primary" @onclick="LoadCatalogAsync">Try again</button>
            <a class="vc-btn" href="">Back to the catalog</a>
        </div>
    </div>
}
else if (Catalog is { } catalog)
{
    @if (Limits is { Enabled: false })
    {
        <div class="vc-alert vc-alert--notice" role="status">
            <h2 class="vc-alert__title">Runs are switched off on this deployment</h2>
            <p class="vc-alert__body">
                The scenarios are still described below and the endpoint still publishes them, but
                this deployment will not execute one. Every button is disabled rather than being
                left to fail, and the descriptions are worth reading either way — each names the
                test that proves the same thing in CI.
            </p>
        </div>
    }

    @if (ScenarioId is { Length: > 0 } requested && Focused is null)
    {
        <div class="vc-empty">
            <span class="vc-eyebrow">404</span>
            <h2 class="vc-empty__title">No demonstration by that name</h2>
            <p class="vc-empty__body">
                <code class="vc-code">@requested</code> is not one of the scenarios this build
                offers. The permalink may predate a rename. These are the ones that exist:
            </p>
            <p class="vc-lab-jump">
                @foreach (var known in catalog.All)
                {
                    <a class="vc-chip" href="@($"lab/{known.Key}")">@known.Heading</a>
                }
            </p>
        </div>
    }
    else if (Focused is { } only)
    {
        <LabScenarioPanel Scenario="only"
                          Detailed="true"
                          Heading="Run this scenario"
                          Result="_state.ResultFor(only.Key)"
                          IsRunning="@(_runningId == only.Key)"
                          IsWaking="_waking"
                          Elapsed="ElapsedSeconds"
                          CanRun="CanRun(only)"
                          Blocked="@BlockedReason(only)"
                          OnRun="RunAsync" />

        <section class="vc-lab-others">
            <h2 class="vc-lab-others__title">The other demonstrations</h2>
            <p class="vc-lab-jump">
                @foreach (var other in catalog.All.Where(candidate => !string.Equals(candidate.Key, only.Key, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)))
                {
                    <a class="vc-chip" href="@($"lab/{other.Key}")">@other.Heading</a>
                }
                <a class="vc-chip" href="lab">All of them, on one page</a>
            </p>
        </section>
    }
    else
    {
        <p class="vc-lab-jump">
            @foreach (var listed in catalog.All)
            {
                <a class="vc-chip" href="@($"#scenario-{listed.Key}")">@listed.Heading</a>
            }
        </p>

        <div class="vc-lab-list">
            @foreach (var listed in catalog.All)
            {
                <LabScenarioPanel Scenario="listed"
                                  @key="listed.Key"
                                  Heading="@listed.Heading"
                                  Result="_state.ResultFor(listed.Key)"
                                  IsRunning="@(_runningId == listed.Key)"
                                  IsWaking="_waking"
                                  Elapsed="ElapsedSeconds"
                                  CanRun="CanRun(listed)"
                                  Blocked="@BlockedReason(listed)"
                                  OnRun="RunAsync" />
            }
        </div>
    }

    <section class="vc-lab-appendix">
        @if (Limits is { } limits)
        {
            <details class="vc-lab-appendix__detail">
                <summary>What this endpoint refuses to do</summary>
                <div class="vc-lab-appendix__body">
                    @if (limits.Policy is { Length: > 0 } limitPolicy)
                    {
                        <p class="vc-lab-appendix__note">@limitPolicy</p>
                    }
                    <dl class="vc-specs">
                        <dt class="vc-specs__key">Runs accepted</dt>
                        <dd class="vc-specs__value">@(limits.Enabled ? "Yes" : "No — switched off here")</dd>
                        <dt class="vc-specs__key">Max shoppers</dt>
                        <dd class="vc-specs__value vc-specs__value--numeric">@limits.MaxParticipants per run</dd>
                        <dt class="vc-specs__key">Concurrent runs</dt>
                        <dd class="vc-specs__value vc-specs__value--numeric">@limits.MaxConcurrentRuns across the whole shop</dd>
                        <dt class="vc-specs__key">Cooldown</dt>
                        <dd class="vc-specs__value vc-specs__value--numeric">@limits.CooldownSeconds s per visitor</dd>
                        <dt class="vc-specs__key">Run budget</dt>
                        <dd class="vc-specs__value vc-specs__value--numeric">@limits.RunTimeoutSeconds s wall clock</dd>
                    </dl>
                </div>
            </details>
        }

        @if (catalog.Payments.Count > 0)
        {
            <details class="vc-lab-appendix__detail">
                <summary>The six ways the simulated gateway can behave</summary>
                <div class="vc-lab-appendix__body">
                    <p class="vc-lab-appendix__note">
                        Rendered from the shop's own <code class="vc-code">PaymentScenarioCatalog</code>
                        rather than restated here, so this table and the one in the docs cannot
                        disagree. A checkout selects one by an explicit hint, or — with no hint — by
                        the trailing cents of the order total.
                    </p>
                    <div class="vc-lab-scroll">
                        <table class="vc-lab-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
                                    <th scope="col">Amount trigger</th>
                                    <th scope="col">Checkout answers</th>
                                    <th scope="col">Afterwards</th>
                                    <th scope="col">Demonstrates</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                @foreach (var payment in catalog.Payments)
                                {
                                    <tr @key="payment.Scenario">
                                        <th scope="row" class="vc-tabular">@payment.Scenario</th>
                                        <td class="vc-tabular">@payment.AmountTrigger</td>
                                        <td>@payment.AuthorizationResult</td>
                                        <td>@payment.Webhooks</td>
                                        <td>@payment.Demonstrates</td>
                                    </tr>
                                }
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </details>
        }

        <p class="vc-lab-appendix__foot vc-tabular">
            GET /@(LabApiClient.CatalogPath) &mdash; the same document this page is built from
        </p>
    </section>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>How long the catalogue fetch is given before the page admits defeat and offers a retry.</summary>
    private static readonly TimeSpan CatalogDeadline = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(45);

    /// <summary>How long a request may run before the page says the shop is asleep. The same two seconds the cart and the checkout use.</summary>
    private static readonly TimeSpan WakeNotice = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2);

    /// <summary>
    /// The client-side budget when the endpoint has not published its own.
    /// <para>
    /// Deliberately longer than any run should take. The server abandons a run on its own budget and
    /// tears the fixture down in a <c>finally</c> either way, so the only thing a short client
    /// deadline buys is a reviewer who is told nothing happened when in fact it did.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private const int FallbackRunSeconds = 120;

    /// <summary>Margin over the server's own budget: a cold start, plus the transcript on the wire.</summary>
    private const int RunDeadlineMarginSeconds = 30;

    /// <summary>How often the running clock and the cooldown countdown repaint.</summary>
    private static readonly TimeSpan Tick = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(250);

    private readonly Stopwatch _clock = new();

    private LabApiClient _api = default!;
    private LabState _state = default!;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _ticker;
    private string? _runningId;
    private bool _waking;
    private bool _catalogLoading;
    private bool _catalogWaking;

    /// <summary>
    /// The permalink's scenario id, or null on <c>/lab</c>.
    /// <para>
    /// Declared as an optional route parameter on one <c>page</c> directive rather than as two, which
    /// is what makes navigating from a permalink back to the index reset it: an unmatched parameter
    /// on a second route directive would keep its previous value and strand the page on a scenario
    /// nobody asked for.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? ScenarioId { get; set; }

    private LabCatalogDocument? Catalog => _state.Catalog;

    private LabLimitsDocument? Limits => Catalog?.Limits;

    /// <summary>The scenario a permalink names, once the catalogue has arrived to name it with.</summary>
    private LabScenarioDocument? Focused => Catalog?.Find(ScenarioId);

    private string DocumentTitle => Focused is { } scenario
        ? $"{scenario.Heading} · Demo lab · Vela Commerce"
        : "Demo lab · Vela Commerce";

    private double ElapsedSeconds => _clock.Elapsed.TotalSeconds;

    /// <summary>The cooldown, from the endpoint's published limits. Zero when it has not said.</summary>
    private int CooldownSeconds => Limits?.CooldownSeconds ?? 0;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _api = new LabApiClient(Http);

        // Resolved rather than injected, so a missing AddStorefrontLab() registration costs
        // transcripts that do not survive navigation instead of a page that throws. See
        // LabServiceCollectionExtensions for the one line that should be in Program.cs.
        _state = Services.GetService<LabState>() ?? new LabState();
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
        {
            return;
        }

        // AFTER the first render, never before it. Awaiting the catalogue in OnInitializedAsync
        // would hold this page blank for as long as a sleeping API takes to answer, which is the
        // spinner-that-looks-broken this whole shop is built to avoid. The shell and the honesty
        // notice paint first, and say what they are waiting for.
        await LoadCatalogAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Fetches the menu once per tab, and again on demand after a failure.
    /// <para>
    /// Reachable twice — from the first render and from Try again — and idempotent in both
    /// directions: a catalogue already in hand short-circuits, and a fetch already in flight is not
    /// duplicated by an impatient second press.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task LoadCatalogAsync()
    {
        if (_state.Catalog is not null || _catalogLoading)
        {
            return;
        }

        _catalogLoading = true;
        _catalogWaking = false;
        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            using var deadline = new CancellationTokenSource(CatalogDeadline);

            var work = _api.GetCatalogAsync(deadline.Token);

            // A call that is slow rather than failing is a cold start, and saying so beats a
            // spinner. Not tied to the deadline token on purpose: a stray two-second timer costs
            // nothing, and cancelling it would mean the notice never appeared on its one path.
            if (await Task.WhenAny(work, Task.Delay(WakeNotice)) != work)
            {
                _catalogWaking = true;
                StateHasChanged();
            }

            _state.Remember(await work);
        }
        catch (LabApiException exception)
        {
            _state.RememberFailure(exception);
        }
        catch (Exception exception)
        {
            // The client turns every documented failure into a LabApiException, so reaching here
            // means something unforeseen. Reported rather than thrown: an unhandled exception would
            // show the Blazor error bar and take the shop's shell down with this page.
            _state.RememberFailure(new LabApiException(
                "The lab's catalogue could not be read.",
                $"{exception.GetType().Name}: {exception.Message}"));
        }
        finally
        {
            _catalogLoading = false;
            _catalogWaking = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Runs one scenario.
    /// <para>
    /// One at a time, which is not merely a nicety: the endpoint serialises runs across the whole
    /// shop, so a second press while one is in flight buys a 429 rather than a second transcript.
    /// The button is disabled for the same reason, and the reason is written under it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task RunAsync(LabScenarioDocument scenario)
    {
        if (_runningId is not null || scenario.Key.Length == 0)
        {
            return;
        }

        var id = scenario.Key;

        _runningId = id;
        _waking = false;
        _state.Forget(id);
        _clock.Restart();
        StartTicker();
        StateHasChanged();

        LabRunResult result;

        try
        {
            using var deadline = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(RunDeadlineSeconds));

            // Never a lowered participant count. A fifty-way race demonstrated with eight is a
            // weaker claim than the one on the button, and the button says fifty.
            var work = _api.RunAsync(id, participants: null, deadline.Token);

            if (await Task.WhenAny(work, Task.Delay(WakeNotice)) != work)
            {
                _waking = true;
                StateHasChanged();
            }

            result = await work;
        }
        catch (Exception exception)
        {
            // The client answers every documented refusal with a result rather than an exception, so
            // this is the unforeseen case. Reported as unreachable because that is what it is from
            // here: no transcript arrived, and the server cleans up after itself regardless.
            result = LabRunResult.Unreachable($"{exception.GetType().Name}: {exception.Message}");
        }
        finally
        {
            _clock.Stop();
            _runningId = null;
            _waking = false;
        }

        _state.Remember(id, result);

        // The endpoint's own Retry-After where it sent one, its published cooldown otherwise. A run
        // that got no answer still spends the visitor's turn on the server, so the countdown starts
        // on every outcome rather than only on the happy one.
        _state.StartCooldown(result.RetryAfterSeconds ?? CooldownSeconds);

        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>The client's deadline: the server's own budget plus a cold start, or a generous fallback.</summary>
    private int RunDeadlineSeconds => Limits is { RunTimeoutSeconds: > 0 } limits
        ? limits.RunTimeoutSeconds + RunDeadlineMarginSeconds
        : FallbackRunSeconds;

    /// <summary>Whether a scenario's Run button is live.</summary>
    private bool CanRun(LabScenarioDocument scenario) =>
        Limits is not { Enabled: false }
        && scenario.Key.Length > 0
        && _runningId is null
        && _state.SecondsUntilReady <= 0;

    /// <summary>
    /// Why the button is disabled, in words, under the button.
    /// <para>
    /// Null while <em>this</em> scenario is the one running, because the notice beside it already
    /// says so and repeating it under the button would read as a second, different problem.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? BlockedReason(LabScenarioDocument scenario)
    {
        if (Limits is { Enabled: false })
        {
            return "This deployment has the lab's runs switched off.";
        }

        if (_runningId is { } running)
        {
            return string.Equals(running, scenario.Key, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
                ? null
                : "Another demonstration is running. The lab takes one run at a time across the "
                  + "whole shop, because two overlapping fifty-way races would exhaust the "
                  + "connection pool the shop serves everybody else from.";
        }

        var wait = _state.SecondsUntilReady;

        return wait > 0
            ? $"Ready in {wait}s. The lab holds each visitor to one run per cooldown — a single "
              + "press becomes up to a hundred and fifty real requests."
            : null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Starts the repaint loop that drives the running clock and the cooldown countdown.
    /// <para>
    /// A timer rather than a CSS animation because both numbers are text a reader is meant to read:
    /// "8.4 s elapsed" on a fifty-way race against a cold container is the difference between a page
    /// that is working and a page that has hung, and an animated bar says neither. It costs one
    /// re-render every 250 ms and only while there is something counting.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void StartTicker()
    {
        if (_ticker is not null)
        {
            return;
        }

        var own = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _ticker = own;

        _ = TickAsync(own);
    }

    private async Task TickAsync(CancellationTokenSource own)
    {
        try
        {
            using var timer = new PeriodicTimer(Tick);

            while (await timer.WaitForNextTickAsync(own.Token))
            {
                await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

                // Repaint first, then stop: the last tick is the one that re-enables the buttons
                // when the cooldown expires, and breaking before it would leave them disabled until
                // something else happened to re-render the page.
                if (_runningId is null && _state.SecondsUntilReady <= 0)
                {
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Disposed while counting. Nothing to recover.
        }
        finally
        {
            if (ReferenceEquals(_ticker, own))
            {
                _ticker = null;
            }

            own.Dispose();
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        try
        {
            _ticker?.Cancel();
        }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException)
        {
            // The loop finished and disposed its own source between the null check and here.
            // Harmless: there is nothing left to cancel.
        }
    }
}
